.TH KOBRA-LAN 1 "2026-11-04" "Kobra LAN CLI" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kobra-lan \- experimental direct Anycubic Kobra X LAN client
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kobra-lan
.RI [ global-options ] " command " [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Inspect G-code locally and perform explicitly reviewed LAN operations with readable text
by default and root --json for the version-1 machine envelope. Upload and print start require separate reviewed execution flags.
Use the help for each command before attempting an operation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --help
Show commands and options. Each command also accepts --help.
.TP
.B --version
Show the application version.
.TP
.BI --config " path"
Select an explicit private configuration; conflicts with --printer.
.TP
.BI --printer " name"
Select a named local profile for this command.
.TP
.BI --state-dir " path"
Select durable state (default ~/.local/share/kobra-lan/uploads).
.TP
.B --json
Emit one schema_version: 1 operational envelope; place before command.
Help, version and completion remain native text.
.SH COMMANDS
.B printer discover [--timeout SECONDS] [--interface IPv4]
performs strictly read-only same-subnet SSDP multicast discovery
(239.255.255.250:1900, ST: ac:3dprinter:fdm) without contacting port 18910 or
9883, auto-pairing, or writing profiles/state. Timeout accepts 1 to 15 seconds
(default 5). Advertisements are unauthenticated and do not verify firmware;
pair explicitly with printer pair --name NAME --host IPv4 and verify the TLS
fingerprint. Rejects global --config and --printer with exit 2.
.B printer pair|list|info|use|remove
manages private access locally and establishes explicit trust.
.B status
and
.B files list|info
are read-only network observations.
.B slots list
is a read-only onboard four-slot filament inventory query over one pinned MQTT
session on Kobra X model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2 (idle or printing) without
state writes; external ACE and multi-box topologies remain unqualified and exit 2.
.B camera status
is a strictly read-only camera inspection over one pinned MQTT session
(info and peripherie) on Kobra X model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2
(experimental: true, hardware_camera_validated: true) without mutating printer
state, opening port 18088, or exposing stream URLs or tokens.
.B camera snapshot --output PATH [--timeout SECONDS] [--overwrite] [--no-start-capture]
captures one JPEG or PNG frame (.jpg, .jpeg, or .png) from the printer's
validated local http://<host>:18088/live/<token> HTTP-FLV stream using an
optional external ffmpeg binary on PATH. Validated FLV bytes are piped via
stdin (stream URLs and tokens never enter argv or output) and the decoded image
is published atomically without clobbering existing files unless --overwrite is
set. Timeout accepts 1 to 60 seconds (default 15). Attempts a direct read-only
HTTP-FLV read first; only if direct read fails and --no-start-capture is omitted
does it send one MQTT video/startCapture and best-effort video/stopCapture on
cleanup. Missing ffmpeg exits 3 before network access.
.B camera pipe [--timeout SECONDS] [--max-bytes BYTES] [--no-start-capture]
streams raw validated HTTP-FLV bytes directly to stdout without external video
binaries, rejecting global --json with exit 2 before target selection. Optional
--timeout accepts 1 to 86400 seconds and --max-bytes accepts 1 to 10737418240
bytes. Uses the same read-only-first and optional startCapture/stopCapture
cleanup strategy. Diagnostics and errors go only to stderr without tokens;
downstream pipe closure exits 0 and foreground interruption exits 130.
.B gcode inspect FILE [--name NAME]
inspects local immutable bytes without configuration or network, reporting
hashes, layer/slicer metadata, and filament/color metadata (paint_info entries,
T<n> tool indices, optional weights, and multicolor). Only recorded
single-filament and 4-color contiguous 1:1 PLA High Speed combinations are
hardware-validated; see compatibility.md.
.B files upload FILE [--name NAME] --sha256 HASH --execute
uploads inspected bytes once; never starts a print and has no implicit dry run.
.B files delete NAME --execute
deletes one root-level file on Kobra X model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2 after
checking for an idle printer with no active project. Use files list to select
the name. The command preserves local profiles, upload and start receipts,
attempt markers, and watch observations. Results include experimental: true
and hardware_delete_validated: true. Bounded listing checks and fresh status
report delete_observed_removed or delete_acknowledged_not_confirmed (exit 0).
A failure after sending begins reports delete_uncertain_no_retry with
available partial evidence (exit 2 for protocol failure, 3 for communication
failure, or 130 for interrupt). Inspect files list before proceeding after
an unconfirmed or uncertain result. The CLI never resends deletion automatically.
.B print plan FILE --name NAME --slot N --auto-leveling on|off --vibration-compensation on|off --flow-calibration on|off [--reprint-from PREVIOUS_ATTEMPT_ID]
reviews a stored print without writes.
.B print start
requires those same inputs plus --plan-sha256 HASH --execute; it rebuilds the
reviewed plan, requires a verified receipt and checks idle before one send.
To reprint a stored file, find the latest eligible local attempt ID with
ordinary print plan or offline jobs list. If it has observed-active evidence and the prior run has ended,
use --reprint-from PREVIOUS_ATTEMPT_ID on both print plan and print start,
and use the separately reviewed reprint hash. A predecessor is consumed at
most once. Never retry an uncertain start or remove its reservation.
.B print watch [--timeout SECONDS]
observes a job already active on Kobra X model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2 over
one pinned MQTT session. The timeout accepts 1 to 259200 seconds and defaults
to 86400. Once an active job is observed, it atomically saves one sanitized
private observation record under --state-dir/observations/<device-prefix>/
without altering existing receipts or markers. See usage.md for outcomes and
qualification evidence.
.B print pause|resume|cancel --execute [--filename NAME]
executes one experimental guarded lifecycle command (wire actions pause,
resume, or stop for cancel) on Kobra X model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2
(experimental: true, hardware_control_validated: true). Omitting --execute or
supplying a malformed --filename exits 2 before target selection or printer
access; there is no implicit dry run. Pre-flight fresh info checks require
printing for pause, paused for resume, and printing, a recognized preparation
phase, or paused for cancel; idle, already-reached, transitional, unrecognized,
or mismatched --filename states exit 2 without publishing. Holds the shared
per-device lease under --state-dir without altering existing profiles, receipts,
markers, or watch observations. Bounded post-ACK polling reports
control_observed_paused|resumed|stopped, control_acknowledged_not_confirmed, or
control_uncertain_no_retry (exit 2 protocol / exit 3 I/O or timeout) with no
automatic retry; inspect status before taking further action.
.B jobs list [--kind all|start|observation] [--limit N]
and
.B jobs info RECORD_ID
inspect local device-scoped job history offline without network access or state
writes. They reconstruct canonical attempt-keyed start receipts/markers,
correlate upload provenance, surface non-canonical, incomplete, or unreadable
records read-only (using unreadable-<storage-key> when no canonical attempt_id
matches the storage key), and list saved unattributed watch observations. Limit
accepts 1 to 500 (default 50). Firmware 2.0.2.2 provides no direct LAN history;
unwatched prints started by other clients are absent and start receipts never
prove completion.
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
generates offline shell scripts without configuration or device queries.
See usage.md for user-level installation; startup files are not modified.
.SH PAIRING
.B kobra-lan printer pair --name bench --default --host 192.0.2.10
provisions an already LAN-enabled Kobra X, model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2.
It queries status without uploading or controlling the printer.
HTTP provisioning is unauthenticated and requires a trusted network even with
--trust-sha256; the fingerprint protects only the later MQTT connection.
On that network, review the observed
TLS fingerprint and explicitly enter y or yes on a terminal; empty input refuses.
For unattended setup, supply --trust-sha256 followed by an independently obtained
64-hex-digit server certificate fingerprint. Mismatch fails without a prompt.
The TLS handshake exchanges the client certificate; MQTT credentials are sent
only after checking the accepted server pin on the subsequent connection.
.PP
Pairing requires --name and rejects global --config and --printer.
Choose another unused name for independent access.
Existing managed destinations, including symlinks, are refused before network access.
The parent must be user-owned, nonsymlink and mode 0700; missing directories are
created privately. Generated PEMs use mode 0600 in a private .pair-ID directory.
The config is published atomically after verification without overwriting.
Retain referenced PEMs if a persistence error leaves a visible config.
Removing an isolated config and only its unreferenced PEM directory does not
revoke access on the printer. Never remove operational attempt markers.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 indicates success (or downstream pipe closure on camera pipe); 2 indicates
argument/protocol/validation failure; 3 indicates configuration/dependency/network
failure. Interrupting printer discover, print watch, camera pipe, or an in-flight
lifecycle control or stored-file deletion returns 130.
Exit 0 for print watch means a result was returned, which can include an
unknown print outcome. Operations are never retried automatically.
.SH FILES
Configuration and state belong to the invoking user. Removing or purging the
package preserves them. Do not delete attempt markers to retry uncertain actions.
.SH SEE ALSO
Installed README.md, NOTICE.md and releases.md in /usr/share/doc/kobra-lan-cli/.
.SH SAVED PRINTER PROFILES
.B printer list
lists local profiles without contacting printers.
.B printer info [name]
shows saved safe metadata.
.B printer use name
selects a locally loadable default without network access.
Global
.B --printer name
and
.B --config path
are mutually exclusive and precede the command. Explicit selection overrides
the saved named default. With no default, explicit selection is required.
No root config is discovered automatically and inventory has no legacy section.
Broken saved defaults never fall back to another printer. Local gcode inspect
requires no configured printer.
.B printer pair --name name --host IPv4 [--default]
creates a new named profile after explicit trust and qualified fresh status.
Omitting --default preserves the prior selection. Named pairing rejects --config.
.B printer remove name [--yes]
requires terminal consent or --yes, detaches registration before owned-file
cleanup and never revokes credentials or removes operational markers. Removing
the default sets default to null and requires explicit selection. Receipts list
deleted and preserved files. Shared, external and ambiguous files are retained.
.SH CONNECTION DIAGNOSIS
.B doctor
uses ordinary target selection and reports configuration, dependencies, HTTP TCP,
MQTT TCP, pinned TLS, MQTT authentication, fresh status and compatibility in order.
Compatibility requires the qualified model name, firmware and valid state.
Optional MQTT device/model IDs must match when supplied; their absence is noted
without failing. Missing required evidence and conflicting values have distinct
diagnostic reason codes. TLS pin verification and authentication remain mandatory.
Each network stage has at most 12 seconds, sharing a 60-second network deadline.
No retries, provisioning, repairs, HTTP requests or printer controls occur.
HTTP TCP failure does not block MQTT. TCP success is not protocol qualification.
A printing target can pass. Only matching model 20030 / firmware 2.0.2.2 is qualified.
The root --json envelope contains schema_version: 1 and ok.
Failures include error.code and error.message; doctor retains partial result.
The inner version 1 diagnostic result contains target, ordered checks and healthy, including
on failure; check fields are stage, status, code, message and nullable next_step.
Exit 0 means healthy; 2 means selection/trust/protocol/compatibility failure;
3 means config/dependency/I/O/network failure and takes precedence when mixed.
Preserve trust and markers; follow safe next steps instead of pairing again.

.SH OUTPUT
Final results and sanitized errors use stdout; prompts, progress, and camera pipe
diagnostics/errors use stderr. camera pipe writes raw binary FLV to stdout and
rejects --json with exit 2.
Errors use invalid_arguments (exit 2), validation_failed (exit 2),
operation_failed (exit 3), or diagnostic_failed (exit 2/3 by stage precedence).
Redirection never changes format. Full file and plan hashes are copyable in
both modes. Upload confirms listing/metadata, not remote byte hashes.
Acknowledged, observed-active and uncertain start states never prove completion.
Preserve reservations and do not resend uncertain operations.
